%I A397960 #9 Aug 27 2026 16:42:49
%S A397960 3,6,9,13,19,26,32,40,49,58,68
%N A397960 a(n) is the maximum number of distinct positive differences of an n-element subset of {0, 1, ..., n*(n-1)/2}.
%C A397960 Equivalently, a(n) = (D(n, n*(n-1)/2) - 1)/2 in the notation of Pikhurko and Schoen, where D(k,m) is the maximum of |A - A| over all k-subsets A of {0, ..., m}. Their difference set contains 0 and both signs, so D = 2*a(n) + 1.
%C A397960 The length m = n*(n-1)/2 is where the two elementary upper bounds on a(n) coincide: the number of unordered pairs is n*(n-1)/2, and the number of available positive difference values in [1, m] is m. Neither bound is slack at this length.
%C A397960 Pikhurko and Schoen proved that for k(m) = (c + o(1))*sqrt(m) the limit d(c) = lim_{m->oo} D(k(m), m)/m exists, and that its value is unknown for 1 < c < sqrt(3). The curve m = n*(n-1)/2 corresponds asymptotically to c = sqrt(2), where their bounds give 1.646 <= d <= 1.788. The terms here are finite-n values on that curve and do not determine the limit: at n = 12 the effective constant n/sqrt(m) is still 1.477 rather than sqrt(2) = 1.4142, and (2*a(n)+1)/m exceeds 1.788 for n <= 11.
%H A397960 O. Pikhurko and T. Schoen, <a href="https://math.colgate.edu/~integers/vol7.html">Integer Sets Having the Maximum Number of Distinct Differences</a>, Integers 7 (2007), #A11.
%e A397960 a(3) = 3: {0, 1, 3} has differences {1, 2, 3}, all three values in [1, 3].
%e A397960 a(5) = 9: {0, 1, 2, 6, 9} has 9 distinct positive differences; the bound 10 is not attained, so no 5-element Sidon set fits in {0, ..., 10}.
%e A397960 a(9) = 32: {0, 1, 2, 3, 14, 21, 26, 30, 36} attains 32 of the 36 available values.
%o A397960 (Python)
%o A397960 def a(n):
%o A397960     m = n*(n-1)//2
%o A397960     best, pos = 0, [0]*n
%o A397960     def rec(d, lo, seen):
%o A397960         nonlocal best
%o A397960         if d == n:
%o A397960             best = max(best, len(seen)); return
%o A397960         rem = (n-d)*(n-d-1)//2 + d*(n-d)
%o A397960         if len(seen) + min(rem, m-len(seen)) <= best: return
%o A397960         for v in range(lo, m - (n-1-d) + 1):
%o A397960             pos[d] = v
%o A397960             rec(d+1, v+1, seen | {v-pos[i] for i in range(d)})
%o A397960     rec(1, 1, set())
%o A397960     return best
%o A397960 print([a(n) for n in range(3, 11)])
%Y A397960 Cf. A003022 (optimal Golomb rulers), A004137 (perfect rulers), A005282 (Mian-Chowla).
%K A397960 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A397960 3,1
%A A397960 _Wujie Gu_, Aug 13 2026
